WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.540
- Here's a joke for ya.

00:00:01.540 --> 00:00:04.090
What did the left eye
say to the right eye?

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:07.337
Between us, something smells
(drum roll then deep breath).

00:00:09.730 --> 00:00:12.170
Donald Trump's presidency changed forever

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:14.280
on November 8, 2018.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.170
Democrats retook control
of the U.S. House,

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:19.130
and, in so doing, ensured
that the second two years

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:22.350
of Trump's presidency are
going to be even more bumpy

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.070
for the President than his first two.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:25.200
And let's be honest,

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.800
those first two years were,
uh you know, bumpy as hell.

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.140
But, Trump ain't seen nothin' yet.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:34.120
Democrats who have been in the
House majority all of a week

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:36.100
are already chomping at the bit

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:38.910
to investigate a President
who, many of them believe,

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:43.810
has run amuck in the White
House for far too long.

00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:46.500
From trying to get Trump's
tax returns publicly released,

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:49.100
to looking into the actions
of his Cabinet secretaries,

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.860
to, yes, impeachment, there are a slew

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:55.200
of potential political
landmines waiting for Trump

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.470
in the House, over the next two years.

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:03.400
So, who will emerge as Trump's
main Democratic antagonist

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.170
between now and 2020?

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:08.110
Here's a look at five names
you really should know.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:11.360
Number one, House Speaker Nancy Pelosi.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:13.830
Yes, yes, yes, I, I get it,

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:16.680
you probably already know
Pelosi's name, but she is,

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:20.190
without any question, the
single most important figure

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:24.260
in Washington these days
not named Donald John Trump.

00:01:24.260 --> 00:01:26.500
Elected Speaker of the
House for the second time

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.160
in her political career,
fun fact, Sam Rayburn

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:31.960
and Henry Clay are the
only other two people

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.920
to be Speaker of the House
in non-consecutive terms.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.580
I did tell you it was gonna be fun.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:38.970
Pelosi does control what
comes to the House floor

00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:40.820
and doesn't, as well
as just how aggressive

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:43.660
her committee chairs are
going to be when it comes

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:46.950
to investigating Trump
and his administration.

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.550
Nothing moves in the new Democratic House

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:54.550
without Pelosi, a, knowing about
it and, b, approving of it.

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:59.490
Number two, New Your
Congressman Jerry Nadler.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:00.850
Now the New York City Congressman,

00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:04.480
he represents the Upper
West Side in Manhattan,

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:06.990
has known Trump for a
very long time, as the two

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:10.510
sort of orbited each other in
the Big Apple political scene.

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:12.700
Trump once even called Nadler, quote,

00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:17.700
one of the most egregious
hacks in contemporary politics.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:20.460
So he's got that going for him.

00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.280
Now Nadler is also the new Chairman

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.260
of the House Judiciary
Committee, which has broad leeway

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:30.040
to investigate any number
of contentious issues,

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:33.370
from voting rights, you
will remember that Trump,

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:35.770
early in his administration,
created a commission

00:02:35.770 --> 00:02:38.050
to look at alleged voter fraud

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:40.720
before disbanding the
commission within months,

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:43.320
to immigration policy put in place

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.420
by this Trump administration.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.730
But Nadler's most powerful
role, without question,

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:51.970
would be to oversee any
effort to impeach Trump,

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:54.000
a process that some
House Democrats believe

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.320
should have started, like, yesterday.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:59.560
Nadler, like Nancy Pelosi,
has been relatively guarded

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:00.393
about whether or not

00:03:00.393 --> 00:03:03.330
Democrats will try to
impeach Trump, though.

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:05.120
In December, he told CNN.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.490
- An impeachment is an attempt
to, in effect, overturn

00:03:09.490 --> 00:03:11.030
or change the result of the last election.

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:14.090
You should do it only for
very serious situations.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:15.670
So that's always the question.

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:20.150
- Number three, California
Congressman Adam Schiff.

00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:21.380
Now for years, the Chairman

00:03:21.380 --> 00:03:23.970
of the House Intelligence
Committee largely operated

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.040
in the shadows, entrusted to work

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.650
with the nation's security
apparatus to counter threats

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:32.060
from both within the
United States and abroad.

00:03:32.060 --> 00:03:35.080
Throughout the ongoing
special counsel investigation,

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:37.000
being led by Robert Mueller, however,

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:39.850
into Russia's interference
in the 2016 election,

00:03:39.850 --> 00:03:41.480
the head of the intelligence committee

00:03:41.480 --> 00:03:45.170
has taken on a much higher profile role.

00:03:45.170 --> 00:03:46.900
And in the 116th Congress,

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:50.330
that gavel is held by,
you guessed, Schiff.

00:03:50.330 --> 00:03:52.530
Now Trump and Schiff do have some history,

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:54.960
in the sense that the
President has bestowed a series

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:56.710
of nicknames on the California Democrat,

00:03:56.710 --> 00:04:01.710
from sleazy Adam Schiff,
to little Adam Schitt.

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.020
Yup, you heard me right.

00:04:05.020 --> 00:04:07.640
Schiffff spent much of the past two years

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:09.670
running down attempts by Republicans

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:12.690
on the Intelligence Committee
to downplay any connections

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:15.190
between Trump's campaign and the Russians.

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:17.550
But now he has the power
to investigate, well,

00:04:17.550 --> 00:04:19.470
almost whatever he wants.

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:22.820
Number four, Massachusetts
Congressman Richard Neal.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:25.460
Okay, when you hear the words
Ways and Means Committee,

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.510
it doesn't exactly conjure up a whole lot

00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:30.480
of excitement and or fireworks.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:31.403
But it should.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:33.940
Because the Ways and Means Committee deals

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:37.140
with all tax-related matters,

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:40.430
and it means you need to
get to know Richard Neal.

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:42.580
Neal, the Chairman of the
Ways and Means Committee,

00:04:42.580 --> 00:04:46.090
is one of only three
people on Capitol Hill

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:47.610
who is authorized to ask

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:50.360
to see President Donald
Trump's tax returns.

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:52.800
Now it's not clear if Neal
would be able to get them,

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:55.350
since Trump has fought like
hell to keep those returns

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:57.060
out of the public eye.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:01.670
But Neal is a guy who can
and will ask for them.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:04.620
Quote, I think the President
has an opportunity here

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:07.130
to defuse this and just release the forms

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:10.230
as every other candidate
for President has done,

00:05:10.230 --> 00:05:12.580
Neal told CNN in November, 2018.

00:05:12.580 --> 00:05:15.690
Quote, even if the request
is made, I don't expect

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:18.850
that afternoon we're going
to see those tax forms.

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:21.530
So, I think we'll do what we have to do,

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:23.440
and then see where the road takes us

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:26.520
and the path that we travel, end quote.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.240
It's a very philosophical answer.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:31.450
Number five, Maryland
Congressman Elijah Cummings.

00:05:31.450 --> 00:05:33.430
Now, outside of Nancy Pelosi,

00:05:33.430 --> 00:05:36.340
Cummings may actually
have the widest latitude

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:38.950
to look into the Trump administration

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:40.300
as Chairman of the House Committee

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:43.100
on Oversight and Government Reform.

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:45.350
Now, that committee, you may remember,

00:05:45.350 --> 00:05:47.600
is where Republicans
conducted an investigation

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:50.930
into the 2012 attacks in Benghazi, Libya.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:53.740
It's the same committee where
Republicans investigated

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:56.690
why the FBI didn't indict Hillary Clinton

00:05:56.690 --> 00:06:01.080
over her decision to use
a private email server.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.440
Now Cummings has already
signaled he wants to look into,

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:05.750
among other things, Trump's
Cabinet secretaries,

00:06:05.750 --> 00:06:08.520
his immigration policies,
and the disbursement

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:11.070
of hurricane relief and recovery dollars.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.870
This is Cummings, quote,
I believe that what we do

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:16.440
in this Congress over the next year or so

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:21.260
will have an impact for
the next 50 to 100 years.

00:06:21.260 --> 00:06:23.060
That's from a CNN interview.

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:26.600
Now, taken together, these five
men and women have the power

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:29.900
to write, or at least co-author,

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:32.990
a new chapter of Trump's presidency.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:36.220
How they approach the
President, and how he reacts

00:06:36.220 --> 00:06:38.910
over the next two years will tell us a lot

00:06:38.910 --> 00:06:42.370
about Trump's chances of
winning again in 2020.

00:06:42.370 --> 00:06:44.900
And that is The Po!nt.

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:47.330
We make these videos every week, twice.

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:51.223
Check in every Tuesday and
Thursday for brand new episodes.

